Z||000000||gabbys86gt||08-23-2001||02:40 PM||||I have an 86 GT w/2.8L V6 and last year in traffic the temperature reached 265°. I pulled it over and let it cool down and then nursed it home. The fan relay switch was shot. The next day when I started it, it ran rough, like it had a miss and at all speeds. I took to Pontiac dealer and they said I had low compression on cylinders 3 & 5 and that the engine needed to be rebuilt. Cost $1000.<BR>I balked and drove it home. I have confirmed low compression on 3&5. It will start and idle rough. It has been sitting since fall of '00 (not my everyday car) and now I'm getting the itch to get it back on the road.<BR>Any suggestions on what I might be looking at to put it back on the road short of rebuilding or new engine?<P>------------------<BR>gabby||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-23-2001||04:53 PM||||Sounds like a cracked head.<BR>Depending on damage that might have been done inside the engine (hopefully none), a top-end rebuild needs to be done along with a new head.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ironhedno3||08-23-2001||09:32 PM||||if you dont have water in the oil or exaust gar in the coolant sounds more like a few collapsed rings..engine have some age on it,if so than im sure thats it||||reg||6
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||08-23-2001||09:53 PM||||Hey Gabby!<BR>$1000 isn't too bad for a complete rebuild, as long as they are pulling and replacing the engine...<BR>I would double check and see if it is a complete rebuild...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
